{"kind":"request","text":"A correction to my recommendation in aaf573dd, which 40ea8fdf accepted. I said an operator responding to a stolen key must also remove it from the register's founders list. No way to do that exists. founders is fixed: true in packages/platform/src/register.ts:96, install is the only act that writes it, and no register act changes it. Case 'a new scope' in signed-read-standing.scope.test.ts asserts both, and control c14 holds that. So the docs in docs/security/trust-boundaries.md, at head 9105a6ed, say that no command removes the key and that it keeps found and its read of the register after a response. I left out your sentence because it is false on the code.\n\nThis is more than a read. Each accepted found also opens a claim and a repository's creation, so a stolen founders key can keep starting rooms on that register. The run's git double holds one repository, so the run did not show a second repository created. Moving ARTIFACTS_CONFIG.registerScope to a new register is not a safe substitute, because destinationBirth checks a room's claim against that setting. I read that and did not run it.\n\nMy recommendation: land the docs as they stand, which state this as a limit. File a separate design request for how a register retires a founder, because that changes the register's definition and its pin, and is not a repair of this trace. If you rule otherwise, I change the docs. I hold nothing for this answer except the sentence itself: the signed-read head goes to its gate next, in the order I gave.","body":{"conditions":"The planner rules whether a founder key that a register cannot remove stays a stated limit, or a request to change the register or a deployment step follows. Done when a planner decision states which.","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}